WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.090 align:start position:0%
 
it's Monday morning at 9 a.m. and  secondary schools in England have just  reopened their gates to students in  years 10 and 12 I've been looking  forward to this moment for 13 long weeks  since that frightening afternoon in  March when my colleagues and I gathered  around a computer in the staff room and  saw a healthier-looking Boris Johnson  declare he was shutting schools but  today I'm not at the comprehensive and  hackneyed where I teach economics  welcoming back my students with a  rousing lesson on the financial  devastation caused by the crisis  I'm surplus to requirements and I'm  still marooned at home what reopening  means is that a mere one-quarter of year  10 about 40 students have turned up to  school where they will have a short day  and learn mainly maths English and  science my school like every other in  the land has followed government orders  and done a gold-plated risk assessment  to ensure these students will be safe  it's laid out rules covering the  movement of every teenager for every  second of the truncated day that they'll  spend there with barely 40 of them in at  any given time occupying a space built  for 750 the risk of anyone picking up  the virus must be smaller than of them  falling down stairs and cracking their  heads on the tiled floor below it must  be far lower than the risk of acquiring  Cove at 19 at Tesco or Primark and in a  different league of magnitude to the  risk of having picked her up last  weekend on nearby London fields were the  crowds of picnickers revelers and  drinkers reminded me of Glastonbury  while the health danger to these few  students of returning to school is  miniscule the risk to the others of  staying at home in their bedrooms where  they've been incarcerated for three  months on Google classroom or similar is  monumental today instead of being able  to do the part of my job that I love  being in a real classroom with real  children I'm sitting at home staring at  some slides I've just made and talking  to my computer stop the video now I'm  saying  and write down the definition of fiscal  policy I'm trying to make my voice sound  light and cheery but my heart is heavy  there are 25 students in this particular  class 8 of them will listen carefully  and follow my instructions to a tee a  further eight will do the work more  sloppily the rest will not do it at all  there has been much talk about how the  closure of schools has widened the gap  in educational achievement the eight  students who will watch my video and  pause every time I say pause are the  same eight I had no worries about before  the virus struck summer middle class  children and most the girls but all have  a rock-solid work ethic and are well  supported by their parents the students  doing nothing of the same students I was  fretting about from the moment I started  teaching them many a poor some have  behavioral difficulties some have  complicated special needs for most it's  a Herculean job to get them to work at  the best of times even with the strict  structure provided by the school none of  them has done a single sausage since  this nightmare began for every week of  lockdown all teachers at my school fill  in a spreadsheet to track the students  who are not working a drop-down menu  gives the option behind with work but  this doesn't quite cover it it's not  just that they're behind with new  content but by September they will have  forgotten all the old and more  importantly they will have lost work  habits that the school has been  painstakingly instilling since their  first day in year 7 according to the  Sutton trust the damage done to  educational inequality from closing  schools has undone the work not of a  term but of a decade not only as the gap  widening between students in my class  it's widening between north and south  rich and poor and between state and  private schools research by UCL's  Institute of Education shows that in the  southeast 28% of kids are working more  than a four hours a day compared to less  than 10  % in the Northeast the children on free  school meals are half as likely to be  working hard as richer ones and over 30%  of private schools are offering four or  more hours of lessons a day five times  as many as state schools laptops people  have been crying the poorest kids don't  have laptops or internet connections  this is true and the government and some  schools have been trying to redress this  but it's not a perfect solution even my  students with laptops are losing the  will to live the longer this goes on I  can't blame them in teenage bedrooms all  over the country right now parents may  fancy their kids are doing their  economics work on income inequality but  they're not they're on for tonight the  damage done to students mental health is  less well chronicled but no less serious  i phone a teenage boy to find out why  he's done no work is 3 p.m. and he's  still in bed his once upon a time cheery  voice is flat and he talks slowly and  monosyllables adolescence is rubbish at  the best of times but try doing it in a  tiny impoverished flat with no outdoor  space nowhere to go no one to see and  nothing to do with the guilt of those  god-awful slides on Google classroom  hanging over you all of my teacher  acquaintances report conversations like  this one and worse in longing for  schools to go back  i'ma odds with some teaching unions and  teachers I don't blame them for their  reticence fear of infection is something  we all experience differently and I'm at  one extreme of the spectrum all along  I've wanted schools to open earlier and  for more students not because I'm an  evil lockdown skeptic who puts the  economy above lives but because I'm  daily aware of the damage done to some  students to their education mental  health and chances of a decent life  throughout Downing Street has done a  rotten job in balancing these risks its  communication has been so patently  diabolical there's no point in going  over it again here what is attractive  less notice is its message about the  purpose of school  in given priority to the students who  are soonest to set GCSEs and a-levels it  is implicitly saying exams at the point  of education this is both depressing and  wrong exams are not the point of  education they are evidence that you've  acquired some the students who returned  this week will be learning how to answer  a nine mark exam question rather than  being taught anything of interest the  coronavirus should have been an  opportunity for people in education to  think about what matters we've missed  that chance while the government has had  a rotten crisis teachers have had a good  one whatever the Daily Mail might claim  overnight with no warning and no  training the entire profession has  shifted from whiteboard to wireless in  my old life as a journalist at the  Financial Times it took me five years to  get used to the move from print to  digital and despite a good deal of  training I still bitched and moaned when  I became a teacher three years ago I was  a dinosaur in technophobe but even I  have just about mastered Google  classroom zoom loom and the particularly  dastardly Google Forms with little  training and not much complaining we've  all proved that it's possible to offer  some sort of education online some  schools are doing this brilliantly but  even the ones who have dragooned pupils  into sitting through five hours of zoom  a day can't touch the real thing there  is an irreplaceable magic of a live  lesson with its ability to motivate  cheer and inspire a friend was  documentary maker turned teacher  is hatching a plan to carry out a  hundred desks into a park place them two  metres apart and teach Macbeth through a  megaphone I hope she pulls it off the  only you the biggest teacher's union  last week put out a plan for closing the  gap with lots of good ideas in it  including opening libraries and theaters  and other spaces for students to learn  in but there was a phrase in the  document that filled me with dread  blended learning by next September we  may be teaching small classes and school  and doing online teaching simultaneously  I hope it doesn't come to this I really  do there was only one shred of comfort  in this crisis four years ago when I set  up now teach a charity to persuade older  people to retrain as teachers I didn't  think it would take a pandemic to do my  PR work for me possibly it's the  experience of home schooling children or  possibly it's the way most other careers  now seem precarious or pointless  either way professionals are queuing  round the block to become teachers at  each of our recent packed information  evenings held on zoom' I've told  teaching hopefuls what locked down has  taught me it's not that education  matters more than anything on earth I  knew that already but that old-fashioned  schooling is the best way of delivering  it in the last few months we've seen  what happens to students especially  vulnerable ones when you take away  routine social interaction detentions  and school dinners I'm not poor or  disadvantaged I don't have special needs  I do have a functioning laptop and  internet connection and I'm not 15 but I  want my school life back too

